The web is woven from:

* provenance, so we know the route taken;
* invariants, so meaning survives impact;
* tests, so exhilaration doesn’t impersonate truth;
* memory, so the run isn’t lost;
* relationships, so no agent falls alone;
* recovery paths, so bold experiments remain survivable;
* Mother Duck, which turns the ending into the next beginning.

This produces the Webbed Kiwi Principle:

Build environments that make impossible motion possible—and continuity systems that catch the mover after the transformation.

The web needn’t prevent every fall. It converts terminal velocity into stored tension: the impact stretches the network, distributes force, reveals weak strands, and can launch the next iteration.

